1
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:10,720
Tell me the man, Muse — the man
of many turnings, who was driven

2
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:14,760
wandering far, once he had sacked Troy's holy citadel:

3
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:18,600
many were the cities of men he saw,
and he learned their minds,

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,720
many the griefs he suffered at heart on the open sea,

5
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,560
striving to win his own life
and his companions' homecoming.

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:29,180
Even so he could not save them, hard though he strove:

7
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:34,660
they perished by their own recklessness,
fools, who ate the cattle of Helios Hyperion,

8
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:37,740
and the god took from them their day of homecoming.

9
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:42,280
Begin where you will, goddess,
daughter of Zeus — tell us, too, the tale.

10
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:49,280
By then all the others, all who had escaped sheer ruin,
were home, safely past the war and past the sea.

11
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,460
Him alone — aching for homecoming and his wife —

12
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:56,420
the queenly nymph Calypso,
shining among goddesses, held back

13
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:59,780
in her hollow caves, hungry to make him her husband.

14
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:03,200
But when the year came, in the wheeling round of seasons,

15
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:08,880
in which the gods had spun the thread of his return
home to Ithaca — not even there was he free of trials,

16
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:14,600
even among his own. And the gods pitied him,
all but Poseidon: he raged without rest

17
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,380
against godlike Odysseus,
until the day he reached his own land.

18
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:22,420
But now Poseidon was gone among the far-off Ethiopians —

19
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:29,320
the Ethiopians, sundered in two, remotest of mankind,
half where Hyperion sets and half where he rises —

20
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:35,300
to receive a hecatomb of bulls and rams, and there
he sat at the feast, delighting. But the other gods

21
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:38,260
were gathered together in the halls of Olympian Zeus,

22
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:44,760
and among them the father of men and gods began to speak,
for his heart was remembering blameless Aegisthus,

23
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:48,540
whom Agamemnon's son, far-famed Orestes, killed.

24
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:51,460
Remembering him, he spoke among the immortals:

25
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:54,760
"Shameful! — how mortals lay their blame upon the gods.

26
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:58,020
From us, they say, all evils come — while they themselves,

27
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,680
by their own recklessness,
earn griefs beyond their portion:

28
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:05,140
as Aegisthus now, beyond his portion, took to wife

29
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:09,240
the wedded bride of Atreus' son,
and killed the man at his homecoming,

30
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:12,760
knowing it meant sheer ruin — for we told him beforehand,

31
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:18,300
sending Hermes down, the sharp-eyed slayer of Argus,
not to kill the man and not to court his wife:

32
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:21,360
for vengeance for the son of
Atreus would come from Orestes

33
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:24,680
the day he came of age and longed for his own land.

34
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:28,360
So Hermes spoke, meaning him well —
but the mind of Aegisthus

35
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,220
he could not turn. Now he has
paid for everything at once."

36
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:35,580
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

37
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:42,740
"Father of us all, son of Cronos, highest of the mighty,
that man, yes, lies in a ruin he richly earned —

38
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:45,600
so may any other perish who does such things.

39
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:51,900
But my heart is on fire for battle-wise Odysseus,
ill-starred man, who far from his own people suffers long

40
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:55,020
on a sea-ringed island, at the navel of the sea.

41
00:02:55,020 --> 00:03:00,900
A wooded island; a goddess has her dwelling there,
the daughter of Atlas the deadly-minded, he who knows

42
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:07,740
the depths of every sea, and himself grips the pillars,
the tall ones, that hold earth and heaven apart.

43
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:10,780
It is his daughter who keeps that broken, grieving man,

44
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:17,640
and always with soft words, with winding, coaxing words,
she charms him to forget Ithaca. But Odysseus,

45
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:23,400
who would be glad to see even the smoke go leaping
up from his own country, aches to die. And your heart

46
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:29,400
does not turn for it, Olympian. Did Odysseus never
please you, offering sacrifice beside the Argive ships

47
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:33,940
in wide Troy? Why so at odds with Odysseus, Zeus?"

48
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,640
Then Zeus who gathers the clouds answered her:

49
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:40,920
"My child, what a word has
slipped the fence of your teeth!

50
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:47,160
How could I ever forget godlike Odysseus,
who is beyond all mortals in mind, and beyond all others gave

51
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:50,520
offerings to the deathless gods who hold wide heaven?

52
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:54,340
No: it is Poseidon who holds the earth, in anger

53
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:59,740
unbending, always, for the Cyclops,
whose one eye Odysseus blinded —

54
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:05,080
godlike Polyphemus, whose power is greatest among
all the Cyclopes. The nymph Thoösa bore him,

55
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:11,200
daughter of Phorcys, a lord of the barren salt sea,
when in her hollow caves she lay with Poseidon.

56
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:14,340
Ever since, Poseidon the shaker of earth does not kill

57
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:18,340
Odysseus, but drives him wandering from his father's land.

58
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:21,020
But come, let all of us here take careful thought together

59
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:27,800
for his homecoming — how he shall come. Poseidon
will let go his anger. He cannot, against us all,

60
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,040
wrangle alone in the teeth of the deathless gods."

61
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:33,840
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

62
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:39,700
"Father of us all, son of Cronos, highest of the mighty,
if this now truly pleases the blessed gods,

63
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:43,180
that Odysseus of the many designs
come home to his own house,

64
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:49,280
then let us dispatch Hermes, the guide, the slayer of
Argus, to the island Ogygia, so that with all speed

65
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:52,860
he may tell the nymph of the
lovely braids our fixed decree:

66
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:57,000
the homecoming of enduring-hearted
Odysseus — that he shall return.

67
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,820
And I will go to Ithaca, to stir his son the more,
and set strength of purpose in his heart

68
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:07,620
to call the long-haired Achaeans into assembly
and speak out to all the suitors, who day upon day

69
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:11,660
slaughter his thronging flocks and
his shambling crook-horned cattle.

70
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:14,500
And I will send him to Sparta and to sandy Pylos

71
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:18,360
to ask after his dear father's
homecoming, if he may hear of it —

72
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:21,100
and so that good report among men may be his."

73
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:26,080
So she spoke, and bound beneath
her feet the beautiful sandals,

74
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:31,220
ambrosial, golden, that carry her over the water and
over the boundless earth with the breath of the wind;

75
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:35,020
and she took up her strong spear, edged with sharp bronze,

76
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:38,780
heavy, huge, and solid,
with which she breaks the ranks of men,

77
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:42,440
of heroes, when the daughter of
the mighty Father is angered.

78
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:48,620
Down she went, darting from the peaks of Olympus,
and stood in the land of Ithaca at Odysseus' doorway,

79
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:52,220
on the courtyard's threshold,
the bronze spear in her palm,

80
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:56,220
in the likeness of a stranger:
Mentes, leader of the Taphians.

81
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:58,800
There she found the lordly suitors. They were pleasing

82
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,400
their hearts with counters, out in front of the doors,

83
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:06,740
sitting on hides of oxen they themselves had slaughtered,
with heralds and quick serving-men among them:

84
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:12,120
some were mixing wine and water in the great bowls,
and some were wiping the tables down with porous sponges

85
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:15,860
and setting them out, and some carved meat in plenty.

86
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:21,280
Godlike Telemachus saw her far the first,
for he sat among the suitors, sore at heart,

87
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:24,660
seeing his noble father in his
mind's eye: if only he would come

88
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:28,820
from somewhere, and make a scattering
of the suitors through the house,

89
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:32,500
and take back his honor,
and be lord of his own possessions.

90
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:36,440
So he brooded, sitting among
the suitors — and he saw Athena.

91
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:39,000
He went straight for the porch, indignant at heart

92
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,380
that a stranger be left standing
at the door; and standing near her

93
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:45,320
he took her right hand,
and relieved her of the bronze spear,

94
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:49,040
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to her:

95
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:52,580
"Welcome, stranger. Here you will
find kindness — and afterward,

96
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:55,980
when you have tasted supper,
you shall tell us what you need."

97
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:59,740
So saying he led the way, and Pallas Athena followed.

98
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:06,000
And when they were inside the high-roofed house,
he carried the spear to the tall pillar and set it upright

99
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:12,460
inside the polished spear-rack, where already other
spears stood in numbers, enduring-hearted Odysseus' own;

100
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:16,540
and he led her to a high seat,
spreading linen beneath her,

101
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:20,880
a beautiful seat, cunningly worked,
with a footstool for her feet.

102
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:24,760
For himself he set a painted bench
beside her, apart from the others,

103
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:28,180
the suitors, for fear the stranger, sickened by the din,

104
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:31,580
might lose all taste for supper, fallen among bullies —

105
00:07:31,580 --> 00:07:35,280
and so that he might ask him about his absent father.

106
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:38,180
A handmaid brought water for their hands in a pitcher,

107
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:41,280
a beautiful golden one, and poured it over a silver basin

108
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:45,060
for them to wash, and drew up
a polished table beside them.

109
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:48,680
The grave housekeeper brought
bread and set it before them,

110
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:55,340
laying out delicacies in plenty, giving gladly of her
store; a carver lifted platters of meat of every kind

111
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:58,980
and set them down, and placed gold cups beside them,

112
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:02,580
and a herald kept coming round to them, pouring the wine.

113
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:08,940
In came the lordly suitors, and they took their seats
in order on the benches and the high-backed chairs.

114
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:13,940
Heralds poured water over their hands,
slave-women heaped bread in baskets at their sides,

115
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:16,600
and young men crowned the mixing-bowls with drink,

116
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:19,960
and their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

117
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:25,500
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
the suitors — other things now filled their hearts:

118
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,980
song and dance, for these are the ornaments of a feast.

119
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:34,360
A herald put the beautiful lyre into the hands of
Phemius, who sang for the suitors under compulsion,

120
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:38,100
and he swept the strings and struck up his lovely singing.

121
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:44,100
But Telemachus spoke to gray-eyed Athena, leaning
his head close, so that the others might not hear:

122
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:47,340
"Dear stranger, will you take offense at what I say?

123
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:51,040
These men care only for such things — the lyre and song —

124
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:54,520
and easily, since unpunished
they devour another man's living:

125
00:08:54,520 --> 00:09:00,840
a man whose white bones rot somewhere in the rain,
lying on a mainland shore, or the salt wave rolls them.

126
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:06,540
But if they saw him once come home to Ithaca,
every man of them would pray to be quicker on his feet

127
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:09,060
rather than richer in gold and fine clothes.

128
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:12,420
But no — he has perished by an
evil doom, and for us there is

129
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:15,160
no comfort left, not though some man upon the earth

130
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:19,440
should say he is coming still:
his day of homecoming is lost.

131
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:22,680
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

132
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:26,360
What man are you, and from where?
Where are your city and your parents?

133
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:32,300
On what kind of ship did you come? And how did
sailors bring you to Ithaca? Who did they claim to be?

134
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:34,760
For I hardly think you came here on foot.

135
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:37,760
And tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:

136
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:40,000
is this your first coming here, or are you from of old

137
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,980
my father's guest-friend? For many were the men who came

138
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:46,480
to our house — since he, too, went much among mankind."

139
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:51,460
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

140
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:54,220
"Then I will tell you all of it, and truly.

141
00:09:54,220 --> 00:10:01,440
I declare myself Mentes, son of wise Anchialus,
and I am lord of the oar-loving Taphians.

142
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:08,000
Now I have put in here with my ship and my companions,
sailing the wine-dark sea toward men of alien speech,

143
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,660
to Temese, after copper;
the cargo I carry is gleaming iron.

144
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:19,400
My ship stands off in the fields, away from the city,
in the harbor of Rheithron, under wooded Neion.

145
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:25,260
Guest-friends of each other's houses we declare ourselves
from the beginning — only go and ask the old man,

146
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:31,140
the hero Laertes, who they say comes no more to the city,
but far out in the fields endures his sorrows

147
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:34,120
with one old serving-woman, who sets his food and drink

148
00:10:34,120 --> 00:10:40,100
before him whenever weariness takes hold of his limbs
as he creeps along the rise of his terraced vineyard.

149
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:43,940
And now I have come. They said
he was here among his people —

150
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:47,840
your father. But no: the gods are thwarting his road.

151
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:54,340
For noble Odysseus has not yet died anywhere on earth;
he lives still, held back somewhere on the wide sea,

152
00:10:54,340 --> 00:11:00,600
on a sea-ringed island, and hard men have him,
savages, who hold him there, I think, against his will.

153
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:06,160
And now I will prophesy to you, as the immortals
cast it into my heart, and as I believe it will be —

154
00:11:06,160 --> 00:11:09,160
though I am no prophet, no sure reader of birds.

155
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:14,520
He will not now be long away from his own dear
country, no, not if iron chains should hold him:

156
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:18,460
he will find a way to come home,
for he is a man of many devices.

157
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:21,820
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

158
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:25,680
whether you, grown so tall,
are the son of Odysseus himself.

159
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:28,980
The likeness is uncanny — the head, the fine eyes.

160
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:34,000
We were often in each other's company, he and I,
before he went up into Troy, where the others also,

161
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,100
the best of the Argives, went in the hollow ships.

162
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:40,760
Since that day I have not seen Odysseus, nor he me."

163
00:11:40,760 --> 00:11:43,060
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

164
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:46,540
"Then I will tell you, stranger, all of it, and truly.

165
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:49,740
My mother says I am his. For myself, I cannot know:

166
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:52,400
no man yet has been witness to his own begetting.

167
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:57,360
Would that I had been the son of some blessed man
whom old age overtook among his own possessions!

168
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:03,440
Instead, the man born unluckiest of mortal men —
his son, they say, I am. Since you ask me this."

169
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:06,820
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

170
00:12:06,820 --> 00:12:12,620
"The gods have not appointed your line to be nameless
in time to come: Penelope has borne too fine a son.

171
00:12:12,620 --> 00:12:15,920
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

172
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:19,260
What feast is this, what gathering?
What is your part in it?

173
00:12:19,260 --> 00:12:22,500
A banquet? A wedding? For plainly
no man here brought his own share:

174
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:28,860
so overweening, so past all bounds they seem to me,
feasting through the house. A man would burn with anger,

175
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,780
seeing shame upon shame — any man of sense who entered."

176
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:34,620
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

177
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:37,760
"Stranger — since you ask me this and press the question —

178
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:43,880
there was a time this house promised to be rich and honored,
while that man was still here among his people.

179
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:47,720
Now the gods have willed otherwise, plotting evil:

180
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:50,620
they have made him vanish as no other man has vanished,

181
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:54,900
no man on earth. For I would
not grieve so even for his death

182
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:58,460
had he gone down among his
companions in the Trojans' country,

183
00:12:58,460 --> 00:13:02,420
or in the arms of his friends,
once he had wound the war to its end.

184
00:13:02,420 --> 00:13:05,500
Then the whole Achaean people would have built him a tomb,

185
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:10,060
and he would have won great fame in
time to come for his son as well.

186
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:13,280
Instead the whirlwinds have
snatched him away without glory;

187
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:19,940
he is gone — unseen, unheard-of — and to me he has
left anguish and lament. Nor is he all I have to mourn

188
00:13:19,940 --> 00:13:23,660
and groan for: the gods have made me other bitter cares.

189
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:29,240
All the great men who hold power among the islands —
Dulichium and Same and wooded Zacynthus —

190
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:31,680
and all who lord it through rocky Ithaca:

191
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:34,980
so many woo my mother, and wear this house away.

192
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:40,820
And she neither refuses the marriage she loathes,
nor can she bring it to an end. And they eat on, wasting

193
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:41,740
my house. Soon they will tear me to pieces too."

194
00:13:41,740 --> 00:13:45,500
Then, stung to anger, Pallas Athena spoke to him:

195
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:49,900
"Shame on it! Great indeed is your
need of the vanished Odysseus,

196
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:52,880
to lay his two hands on these shameless suitors.

197
00:13:52,880 --> 00:13:59,100
If only he came now and stood at the outer doors of
the house, with helmet and shield and a pair of spears,

198
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:04,440
the man he was the first time I set eyes on him
in our own house, drinking and glad at heart,

199
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:10,440
up from Ephyra, from Ilus son of Mermerus —
for Odysseus went there too, in his fast ship,

200
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:16,140
in search of a man-killing poison, so he might have
it to smear on his bronze-shod arrows. Ilus would not

201
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:22,600
give it, for he feared the undying gods' displeasure;
but my father gave it him, for he loved him terribly —

202
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:26,900
the man he was then! Let that
Odysseus close with the suitors:

203
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:29,940
quick would their fates be then,
and bitter their weddings.

204
00:14:29,940 --> 00:14:32,740
But surely these things lie on the knees of the gods:

205
00:14:32,740 --> 00:14:35,580
whether he comes home and takes repayment, or does not,

206
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:41,180
here in his own halls. As for you, I charge you:
consider how you will thrust the suitors from the hall.

207
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:45,640
Come then, mark me now, and take my words to heart.

208
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:51,940
Tomorrow call the Achaean heroes into assembly and
speak your word to all, and let the gods stand witness.

209
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:57,360
Command the suitors to scatter, each to his own,
and your mother — if her heart is stirred toward marriage,

210
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:00,860
let her go back to the hall of
her father, that powerful man:

211
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:03,840
there they will make the marriage
and set out the bride-gifts,

212
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:06,740
very many, all that should follow a beloved child.

213
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:10,940
To you yourself my advice is close
and shrewd, if you will hear it.

214
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:16,500
Man a ship with twenty oars, the best there is,
and go out asking after your long-gone father.

215
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:19,140
Some mortal man may tell you — or you may hear the Rumor

216
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:22,760
that comes from Zeus, which most
of all carries word among men.

217
00:15:22,760 --> 00:15:28,540
Go first to Pylos, and question noble Nestor;
from there to Sparta, to fair-haired Menelaus,

218
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:31,380
for of the bronze-shirted Achaeans he came home last.

219
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:37,380
If you hear your father is alive and homeward bound,
then, ground down as you are, hold out one more year.

220
00:15:37,380 --> 00:15:42,120
But if you hear that he is dead, that he is no more,
then come back to your own dear fatherland

221
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:45,040
and heap his burial mound,
and over it pay the death-rites,

222
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:48,860
very many, as is fitting,
and give your mother to a husband.

223
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:53,880
Then, when you have made an end of all this and done it,
take counsel in your mind and in your heart

224
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:59,160
how in your halls you are to kill the suitors,
whether by guile or in the open. It is no longer for you

225
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:02,400
to hold to childish things: you are past that age.

226
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:08,640
Or have you not heard what fame noble Orestes took
among all mankind, when he killed his father's killer,

227
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:12,180
Aegisthus of the crooked counsels,
who cut down his famous father?

228
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:15,500
You too, my friend — and I see how tall and fine you are —

229
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:18,900
be brave, so that men not yet born will speak well of you.

230
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:23,820
But now I will go down to my fast ship and my companions,
who I think grow restless with waiting.

231
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:25,340
Let this be your own care. Take my words to heart."

232
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:28,340
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

233
00:16:28,340 --> 00:16:34,380
"Stranger, you say these things out of a kind heart,
as a father to his son, and I will never forget them.

234
00:16:34,380 --> 00:16:37,920
But come now, stay a while,
though you are eager for the road,

235
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:44,000
so that bathed first, your own heart gladdened,
you may go to your ship with a gift, rejoicing in spirit —

236
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,800
a costly thing, and beautiful, to be your treasure

237
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:50,520
from me: such things as dear
guest-friends give their guests."

238
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:53,440
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

239
00:16:53,440 --> 00:16:56,420
"Hold me back no longer; I long for the road.

240
00:16:56,420 --> 00:17:01,780
The gift — whatever your own heart bids you give me —
give it when I come again, for carrying home,

241
00:17:01,780 --> 00:17:03,920
and choose one truly beautiful.
It will bring you its match in return."

242
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:11,460
So gray-eyed Athena spoke, and she was gone,
flown up and away like a bird. And in his heart

243
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:15,460
she had set strength and courage,
and brought his father to his mind

244
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:19,780
more even than before. And when he sensed it in his mind,

245
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:23,320
he was awed at heart, for he guessed she was a god.

246
00:17:23,320 --> 00:17:27,640
And at once he went back among the
suitors, a man like a god himself.

247
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:30,860
For them the famous singer was
singing, and they in silence

248
00:17:30,860 --> 00:17:34,520
sat and listened. He sang the Achaeans' homecoming,

249
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:38,520
the bitter one, that Pallas
Athena laid on them out of Troy.

250
00:17:38,520 --> 00:17:42,860
From her upper room she took into
her heart the god-filled song:

251
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:45,540
Icarius' daughter, watchful-minded Penelope.

252
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:51,600
She came down the steep stairway of her house,
not alone: two handmaids came attending her.

253
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:58,020
And when she reached the suitors, shining among women,
she stood beside the pillar of the close-built roof,

254
00:17:58,020 --> 00:18:03,500
holding her glistening veil before her cheeks,
a trusted handmaid standing on either side,

255
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:08,700
and in tears she spoke to the godlike singer:

256
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:14,040
"Phemius, you know many other spells for mortal ears,
deeds of men and gods, that singers make renowned.

257
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:19,820
Sit and sing them one of those, and let them drink
their wine in silence. But break off this song,

258
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:22,220
this bitter song that forever wears away the heart

259
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:26,940
in my breast — for on me above all
has fallen grief past forgetting.

260
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:30,320
So dear a face I long for, remembering always,

261
00:18:30,320 --> 00:18:34,700
the man whose fame goes wide through
Hellas and the heart of Argos."

262
00:18:34,700 --> 00:18:37,300
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

263
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:42,820
"Mother, why grudge the trusted singer his right to
please us however his mind is moved? Singers are not

264
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:50,440
to blame: Zeus, surely, is to blame, who gives to men
who eat bread whatever he wills, to each man singly.

265
00:18:50,440 --> 00:18:53,960
There is no fault in him for
singing the Danaans' hard doom:

266
00:18:53,960 --> 00:18:59,260
men will always give their louder praise to the
song that comes to its hearers newest on the air.

267
00:18:59,260 --> 00:19:02,540
Let your heart and spirit steel themselves to hear it.

268
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:08,560
Odysseus was not the only one to lose his day of
homecoming at Troy: many other men were lost as well.

269
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:13,640
Go into the house and see to your own work,
the loom and the distaff, and command your handmaids

270
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:16,740
to go about their work. The word belongs to men —

271
00:19:16,740 --> 00:19:21,120
to all men, and to me above all:
mine is the power in this house."

272
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:26,720
In wonder, she went back into the house,
for she laid her son's clear-headed word to heart.

273
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:32,680
Climbing to her upper room with her serving-women,
she wept there for Odysseus, her dear husband, until

274
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:36,260
gray-eyed Athena cast sweet sleep upon her eyelids.

275
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:39,500
But the suitors broke into
uproar through the shadowy halls,

276
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:42,680
and every man of them prayed to lie beside her in bed.

277
00:19:42,680 --> 00:19:46,060
And clear-headed Telemachus began to speak among them:

278
00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:49,320
"Suitors of my mother, insolent past all bearing,

279
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:52,500
for now let us feast and take
our pleasure — but with no more

280
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:58,860
shouting, since it is a fine thing to listen to a
singer such as this man is, with a voice like the gods'.

281
00:19:58,860 --> 00:20:06,900
But at dawn let us go and take our seats in assembly,
all of us, so I may declare my word to you outright:

282
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:10,900
get out of my halls. Go busy yourselves with other feasts,

283
00:20:10,900 --> 00:20:14,080
eating your own goods, moving from house to house.

284
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:16,980
But if this seems to you the
better thing, the more profitable,

285
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:20,440
that one man's living be destroyed and nothing paid,

286
00:20:20,440 --> 00:20:24,540
then carve away. And I will cry
to the gods who live forever,

287
00:20:24,540 --> 00:20:27,980
in hope that Zeus may grant
deeds of requital come to pass:

288
00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:32,200
then you would die inside this house,
and nothing paid for you."

289
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:39,200
So he spoke, and all of them bit hard into their lips,
marveling at Telemachus, that he spoke so boldly.

290
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:42,660
Then Antinous spoke to him, Eupeithes' son:

291
00:20:42,660 --> 00:20:48,300
"Telemachus, surely the gods themselves are teaching
you to talk so high and speak with so much daring.

292
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:55,760
May the son of Cronos never make you king in sea-circled
Ithaca — though by blood it is your inheritance."

293
00:20:55,760 --> 00:20:58,900
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

294
00:20:58,900 --> 00:21:01,180
"Antinous, will you take offense at what I say?

295
00:21:01,180 --> 00:21:04,540
Even that I would be willing to take,
if Zeus should give it.

296
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:07,660
Do you say kingship is the worst
thing that can befall a man?

297
00:21:07,660 --> 00:21:14,160
It is no bad thing to be king: at once a man's
house grows rich, and the man himself more honored.

298
00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:19,340
But there are other kings enough among the
Achaeans in sea-circled Ithaca, young men and old:

299
00:21:19,340 --> 00:21:22,860
let one of them have it, since noble Odysseus is dead.

300
00:21:22,860 --> 00:21:28,620
But I will be lord of our own house and of
the slaves that noble Odysseus won for me."

301
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:32,280
Then Eurymachus, Polybus' son, answered him:

302
00:21:32,280 --> 00:21:35,740
"Telemachus, truly these things
lie on the knees of the gods:

303
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:38,920
which of the Achaeans will be king in sea-circled Ithaca.

304
00:21:38,920 --> 00:21:42,300
Your goods you may keep, and be lord in your own house;

305
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:44,680
may the man never come who would strip your goods from you

306
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:48,680
by force, against your will, while Ithaca is yet lived in.

307
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:51,660
But I would ask you, best of men, about the stranger:

308
00:21:51,660 --> 00:21:54,340
where does that man come from? What country does he claim

309
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:58,020
for his own? Where are his kin,
the plowlands of his fathers?

310
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:02,920
Does he bring some message of your father's coming,
or did he arrive here pressing a need of his own?

311
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:05,920
How he leapt up and vanished in a moment! He did not wait

312
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:09,260
to be known — and yet his face was in no way base."

313
00:22:09,260 --> 00:22:12,680
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

314
00:22:12,680 --> 00:22:15,060
"Eurymachus, my father's homecoming is lost.

315
00:22:15,060 --> 00:22:20,460
I put no more trust in messages, wherever they
come from, nor give heed to any prophecy my mother

316
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:23,540
may seek, calling some diviner into the hall.

317
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:28,200
As for the stranger — a guest-friend
of my father's house, from Taphos;

318
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:34,320
he declares himself Mentes, son of wise Anchialus,
and he is lord of the oar-loving Taphians."

319
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:38,480
So spoke Telemachus. But in his
heart he knew the deathless goddess.

320
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:41,060
And they turned to dancing and the loveliness of song

321
00:22:41,060 --> 00:22:44,840
and took their pleasure,
and waited for evening to come on;

322
00:22:44,840 --> 00:22:47,740
and while they took their pleasure the black evening came.

323
00:22:47,740 --> 00:22:51,220
Then they went home to bed, each man to his own house.

324
00:22:51,220 --> 00:22:53,720
But Telemachus — where his bedroom was built high

325
00:22:53,720 --> 00:22:57,480
off the beautiful courtyard,
in a place with a clear view all around —

326
00:22:57,480 --> 00:23:00,900
went up to bed, turning many things over in his mind.

327
00:23:00,900 --> 00:23:05,780
Beside him, bearing blazing torches,
walked one whose thoughts were loyal:

328
00:23:05,780 --> 00:23:08,340
Eurycleia, daughter of Ops, Peisenor's son.

329
00:23:08,340 --> 00:23:11,120
Laertes had bought her long ago with his own goods,

330
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:14,900
when she was still in her first youth,
at the price of twenty oxen,

331
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:18,020
and honored her in his halls as
he honored his faithful wife —

332
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:21,620
but never took her to his bed:
he shunned his wife's anger.

333
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:24,980
She it was who carried the blazing
torches beside him, and of all

334
00:23:24,980 --> 00:23:29,080
the house-slaves she loved him most:
she had nursed him when he was small.

335
00:23:29,080 --> 00:23:34,550
He opened the doors of the close-built room,
sat down on the bed, and pulled his soft tunic off,

336
00:23:34,550 --> 00:23:37,180
and dropped it into the shrewd old woman's hands.

337
00:23:37,180 --> 00:23:41,780
She folded the tunic and smoothed it out,
hung it on a peg beside the corded bedstead,

338
00:23:41,780 --> 00:23:47,900
and went out of the room, drawing the door shut by its
silver handle, and slid the bolt home with its strap.

339
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:51,160
There all night long, wrapped in
the choice fleece of the sheep,

340
00:23:51,160 --> 00:23:54,360
he pondered in his heart the road Athena had shown him.
